WEBVTT

00:00:00.912 --> 00:00:06.049
This is the full flow, not a mock-up, and it is explained step by step. Four

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:11.456
containers talk over real network calls: the payment bridge I built, a database,

00:00:11.456 --> 00:00:16.660
a simulator of a hotel PMS in the style of the Oracle Hospitality Integration

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.932
Platform, and a simulator of a card processor. Everything is synthetic. First,

00:00:21.932 --> 00:00:23.419
the problem it solves.

00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:28.815
Why it exists. One hotel payment has to agree across three systems: the PMS, the

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:33.473
card processor and the guest's card. Things go wrong in five ways. A retry can

00:00:33.473 --> 00:00:38.192
charge a card twice. A reply that arrives too late can post a payment twice. An

00:00:38.192 --> 00:00:42.731
outage can leave money charged but never on the folio. Card numbers can leak

00:00:42.731 --> 00:00:47.329
into logs. And the books may not match what the processor settles. The bridge

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:50.853
answers each one, and the video shows every answer working.

00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:57.787
Who is who. Before the code, the people and systems in one hotel card payment.

00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:02.578
The guest pays. The hotel's front desk runs a property management system, here

00:01:02.578 --> 00:01:07.246
OPERA, which holds the guest's bill, the folio. In the middle is the payment

00:01:07.246 --> 00:01:12.036
bridge, the part I built. It sends the card to a payment processor. Behind the

00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:16.766
processor are three parties the bridge never calls: the acquirer, the hotel's

00:01:16.766 --> 00:01:21.495
bank for cards; the card network, such as Visa or Mastercard; and the issuer,

00:01:21.495 --> 00:01:26.224
the guest's own bank, which says yes or no. When the guest pays, the question

00:01:26.224 --> 00:01:31.137
travels all the way to the issuer and back in about two seconds, and the guest's

00:01:31.137 --> 00:01:35.560
bank holds the money. The money itself moves one to three days later, at

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.289
settlement: from the issuer, through the network, to the acquirer, which pays

00:01:40.289 --> 00:01:45.202
the hotel less the fees. In this demo, one simulator plays the processor and the

00:01:45.202 --> 00:01:46.676
three parties behind it.

00:01:48.206 --> 00:01:52.807
Here is the picture. A booking reaches the bridge as an event. The bridge

00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:57.786
applies a payment rule and creates a payment link. The guest pays, and the card

00:01:57.786 --> 00:02:02.828
is tokenised the moment it arrives: only a token comes back, and only ciphertext

00:02:02.828 --> 00:02:07.681
is stored. The bridge asks the processor to authorise and capture, then posts

00:02:07.681 --> 00:02:12.597
the payment to the guest's folio in the PMS, and tells the hotel's own systems

00:02:12.597 --> 00:02:17.576
with a signed webhook. Every step carries an idempotency key, and every failure

00:02:17.576 --> 00:02:22.240
ends in one of two places: posted, or kept in a dead letter queue with the

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.832
reason. Now let me show it, and then break it on purpose.

00:02:27.338 --> 00:02:32.314
First, the happy path. The PMS front desk takes a new reservation and announces

00:02:32.314 --> 00:02:37.353
it to the bridge. Here the simulator posts the event over HTTP; real OPERA Cloud

00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:42.204
would deliver it on OHIP's streaming API. The bridge answers with the payment

00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:46.865
rule: advance purchase, the full amount within twenty four hours. Note the

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:51.778
business date at the top. In a real property it moves only at night audit, not

00:02:51.778 --> 00:02:52.534
at midnight.

00:02:54.103 --> 00:02:59.298
In the bridge, a payment request now exists. The guest tokenises a test card and

00:02:59.298 --> 00:03:04.428
pays. The card number is encrypted on arrival, and the guest sees only a token.

00:03:06.032 --> 00:03:11.012
On the processor side there is an authorisation and a capture, and only the last

00:03:11.012 --> 00:03:15.991
four digits of the card. Behind the processor, the simulator also plays the card

00:03:15.991 --> 00:03:20.535
network, here Visa, and the guest's bank, which approved with a code. The

00:03:20.535 --> 00:03:25.140
processor deduplicates on an idempotency key, so a repeat can never charge

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:25.514
twice.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:31.487
And at the front desk, the payment is on the guest's folio. I open the

00:03:31.487 --> 00:03:36.534
reservation: folio window one, a payment code, a cashier, eight hundred dollars,

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:41.076
our reference and the processor's reference. It is a credit, posted as a

00:03:41.076 --> 00:03:45.493
payment, not as a charge, so the folio shows the stay paid in advance.

00:03:46.178 --> 00:03:51.238
The bridge also tells the hotel's own systems what happened. Payment captured,

00:03:51.238 --> 00:03:56.298
and payment posted, each arrive as a signed webhook. The receiving side checks

00:03:56.298 --> 00:04:01.163
the signature before it trusts the message, and every event has an id, so a

00:04:01.163 --> 00:04:04.407
repeat is ignored. The card number is never in it.

00:04:05.028 --> 00:04:09.994
Edge case one: the same booking event arrives again after the guest has paid.

00:04:09.994 --> 00:04:15.025
The bridge does not create a new request, and does not ask for money twice. It

00:04:15.025 --> 00:04:20.121
flags the booking for a refund or balance review, because a changed total after

00:04:20.121 --> 00:04:21.540
payment needs a human.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:27.401
Edge case two: a lost reply. This PMS does not deduplicate, like the real world.

00:04:27.401 --> 00:04:32.309
I make it record the next payment and then answer too late. The bridge times

00:04:32.309 --> 00:04:37.282
out, but before it retries it looks for its own reference, finds the payment,

00:04:37.282 --> 00:04:41.157
and does not post twice. One new line on the folio, not two.

00:04:41.764 --> 00:04:47.057
Edge case three: the PMS goes down after the card is charged. The bridge says so

00:04:47.057 --> 00:04:52.283
plainly and keeps the payment in a dead letter queue with the reason. The money

00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:57.444
is not lost, and it is not hidden. When the PMS is back, one click replays it,

00:04:57.444 --> 00:04:59.428
and it is posted exactly once.

00:05:01.181 --> 00:05:05.698
Edge case four: the processor approves the authorisation, then declines the

00:05:05.698 --> 00:05:10.517
capture. The bridge does not leave the hold on the card. It releases it, and you

00:05:10.517 --> 00:05:15.335
can see the void in the processor ledger. The payment link goes back to open, so

00:05:15.335 --> 00:05:18.226
the guest can try again, and this time it works.

00:05:20.471 --> 00:05:25.677
Edge case five: night audit runs and the business date moves, and I also expire

00:05:25.677 --> 00:05:30.686
the access token. The next payment makes the processor capture the money and

00:05:30.686 --> 00:05:35.893
then go quiet. The bridge fetches a new token, reads the new business date from

00:05:35.893 --> 00:05:40.309
the PMS, retries with the same key, and the processor charges once.

00:05:46.587 --> 00:05:51.547
At the end of the day, finance reconciles. The bridge downloads the settlement

00:05:51.547 --> 00:05:56.379
file, what the acquirer paid out, and matches every capture against what was

00:05:56.379 --> 00:06:01.467
posted. Five captures, five matches. Now a messy statement: one capture missing,

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:06.490
one settled short, one we never made. Each exception has a name finance can act

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:06.681
on.

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:12.217
The operator log shows all of this with the card number masked. A mask catches

00:06:12.217 --> 00:06:17.070
mistakes, but the code never logs the number in the first place, and the vault

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:21.736
view shows ciphertext, never the number. Every call that changes something,

00:06:21.736 --> 00:06:26.652
every refusal, and every time a card is opened also go to an audit trail, which

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:29.078
the application refuses to run without.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:36.231
How do I know the other cases hold? 612 automated tests, every one passing, and

00:06:36.231 --> 00:06:41.822
a coverage report of 99.4 percent of lines. The important tests were checked the

00:06:41.822 --> 00:06:47.204
hard way: I put each bug back on purpose, 115 of them, and every one made its

00:06:47.204 --> 00:06:52.515
test fail. Standard tools, Checkstyle and ArchUnit, hold the coding rules on

00:06:52.515 --> 00:06:57.897
every build. A page in the repository lists every edge case and the test that

00:06:57.897 --> 00:07:01.322
fails if it breaks, and also what is not covered.

00:07:02.942 --> 00:07:08.124
How it should work in production. On AWS, the bridge runs as two or more tasks

00:07:08.124 --> 00:07:13.371
in private subnets, behind a load balancer and a web application firewall, with

00:07:13.371 --> 00:07:18.686
PostgreSQL on RDS, keys in KMS, and fixed outbound addresses for OPERA Cloud and

00:07:18.686 --> 00:07:23.734
the acquirer. The runbook is written, but not yet run in an account. Booking

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:28.982
events would come from OHIP's streaming API, which delivers at least once, with

00:07:28.982 --> 00:07:33.897
no acknowledgement and no dead letter queue, so the bridge would store its

00:07:33.897 --> 00:07:38.614
offset and skip an event it has already seen. The processor becomes the

00:07:38.614 --> 00:07:43.862
acquirer's checkout, with 3-D Secure and hosted card entry, so a guest's number

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:48.777
never reaches this service. Authentication is already in: OAuth2 tokens or

00:07:48.777 --> 00:07:54.092
hashed API keys, each limited by scope. Still to add: tenants for many operators

00:07:54.092 --> 00:07:59.273
and properties, a sweeper for crashes between steps, and chargeback cases with

00:07:59.273 --> 00:08:00.402
scheme deadlines.

00:08:01.032 --> 00:08:06.257
That is the full flow: idempotent charges, a repeated event, a lost reply, an

00:08:06.257 --> 00:08:11.277
outage and a replay, a released hold, a token refresh, and reconciliation,

00:08:11.277 --> 00:08:16.501
against separate systems. The PMS here is a simulator I wrote in the shape of

00:08:16.501 --> 00:08:21.861
the Oracle Hospitality Integration Platform. It is not Oracle software, and the

00:08:21.861 --> 00:08:27.017
real thing needs Oracle's own sandbox, which is the next step. The code, the

00:08:27.017 --> 00:08:32.173
tests and the log of what the agents got wrong are in the public repository.
